{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["And the sense of <b>for she was a shepherdess</b> is to indicate that Laban's flock had no other shepherd besides her, for her father entrusted the flock to her alone and she alone tended them all the days; Leah her sister did not go out with them at all. And her situation was not like that of the daughters of Jethro, who were seven daughters all of them shepherdesses together, as it says, \"And they came and drew\" (Exodus 2:16). And perhaps because Leah's eyes were tender, the sun would harm her, or because Leah was older and fit for a man and her father was concerned about her, but Jethro was prominent in his place and priest of the land, and people feared to approach his daughters. Or perhaps Laban was more modest than him, for the family of Abraham was proper and modest, and Rachel was young and there was no concern about her. And this is the significance of \"And Jacob kissed Rachel\" — or it is as the words of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 27:27), that a kiss with a lamed is not on the mouth; rather, he kissed her on her head or on her shoulder:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:36:23.945287392Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}